<DocsLayout title="Expo EAS" activeNav="/platform/integrations/eas">
  <h1>Expo EAS</h1>
  <p>
    The Expo integration connects an Expo account, links its projects to applications here, and keeps
    identifiers, credentials and builds in step between the two systems. It is the one integration
    where both sides hold overlapping state, so most of this page is about which side owns what.
  </p>

  <h2>Two scopes: the account and the project link</h2>
  <p>
    This distinction runs through the whole feature, including the CLI:
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Scope</th><th>Belongs to</th><th>Holds</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Expo account</strong></td>
        <td>An integration in the workspace</td>
        <td>The access token, and the list of projects that account can see</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Project link</strong></td>
        <td>One application</td>
        <td>Which Expo project this app is, and its sync state</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Connecting an account does not link anything by itself, and unlinking one app leaves the account
    connected for the others.
  </p>

  <h2>Connecting an account</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Connect Expo</strong> takes an Expo access token. If the token can see more than one
    account, you also choose which one. The token is encrypted at rest and used for every later call —
    listing projects, syncing, provisioning credentials.
  </p>
  <p>
    Unlike GitHub and GitLab, this connection is <em>not</em> browser-only: it is a token, so
    <a href="/cli/integrations/eas"><code>appmctl eas connect</code></a> does the same job from
    a script.
  </p>

  <h2>Creating or linking a project</h2>
  <p>
    Each application has an Expo screen (<strong>Expo EAS</strong> in the sidebar) that handles
    linking. Two paths are available:
  </p>

  <Screenshot
    src="expo-app-setup"
    alt="The Expo setup form with two options: Create a new project and Link an existing project"
    caption="The setup screen for an unlinked application. Choose between creating a new Expo project or linking to one that already exists."
  />

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Create a new project</strong> — creates <code>@&lt;account&gt;/&lt;app-slug&gt;</code>
      on Expo, pushes the app's identifiers and supported credentials, and links the project. This is
      the right choice when Expo is new for this application.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Link an existing project</strong> — picks from projects the connected account can see
      that are not yet linked to another application. Optionally syncs the display name and description
      from Expo into App Manager.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Both paths create a one-to-one link: each Expo project can be linked to at most one application,
    and each application can hold at most one Expo project.
  </p>

  <h2>The linked application</h2>
  <p>
    Once linked, the Expo screen transforms into a dashboard for the connection. The header shows
    the project name, its full Expo path (<code>@account/slug</code>), and provides buttons to open
    the project on expo.dev, pull changes, or unlink.
  </p>

  <Screenshot
    src="expo-app-linked"
    alt="Linked Expo screen showing project name, LINKED badge, automatic sync section, stats, EAS workflows, credentials, and activity tabs"
    caption="A linked application: project header, automatic sync toggle, credential sets, and EAS activity."
  />

  <h3>Automatic sync</h3>
  <p>
    The <strong>Automatic sync</strong> section controls a one-way push from App Manager to Expo.
    When enabled, local changes to the app's name, icon, identifiers, and supported credentials are
    pushed to Expo automatically. Expo never pushes back — use <strong>Pull from Expo</strong> when
    you want to import remote changes.
  </p>
  <p>
    The sync status indicator shows whether the push is idle, queued, syncing, or has failed. A
    failed sync displays the error message and can be retried by toggling the switch off and on.
    Automatic sync cannot be enabled while the project is missing upstream or the account's token is
    broken — the platform refuses rather than silently enabling something that cannot work.
  </p>

  <h3>EAS Workflows</h3>
  <p>
    When the Expo project has a GitHub repository connected, this section shows the workflow files and
    pipeline configuration state. If GitHub is not yet connected, a button links directly to Expo's
    settings to configure it.
  </p>

  <h3>EAS Credentials</h3>
  <p>
    The credentials section shows metadata only — secret key material is never returned to this page.
    For each platform (Android, iOS) it lists the signing profiles and whether a submit key is
    configured. To upload a local credential from the <a href="/platform/credentials-vault">vault</a>,
    open an app identifier and use <strong>Expo</strong> on that credential.
  </p>

  <h3>EAS Activity</h3>
  <p>
    The activity section has two tabs: <strong>Builds</strong> and <strong>Submissions</strong>.
    Builds created by EAS CLI, EAS triggers, or an App Manager pipeline appear here with their
    status, platform, and version. From this table you can inspect, cancel, retry, and submit builds
    to app stores. Submissions track the Google Play and App Store delivery status.
  </p>
  <p>
    Both tabs support importing builds into App Manager — select the builds you want and click
    <strong>Import selected builds from EAS</strong>. Already-imported builds are marked with a
    checkmark.
  </p>

  <h2>Workspace-level view</h2>
  <p>
    The <strong>Integrations &gt; Expo EAS</strong> tab in workspace settings shows all connected
    Expo accounts and their projects. From here you can sync the project list, import projects in
    bulk, or disconnect an account.
  </p>

  <Screenshot
    src="integrations-eas-linked"
    alt="Workspace Integrations page showing a connected Expo account with one linked project"
    caption="The Expo EAS tab after connecting an account and linking a project. Each project shows its local link status."
  />

  <p>
    <strong>Import</strong> creates an App Manager application from an Expo project — the quick path
    when Expo is already your source of truth. Projects can be imported in batches, which matters
    when an account holds dozens.
  </p>

  <h2>Identifiers</h2>
  <p>
    The identifier view compares what this platform holds against what Expo holds, per platform, and
    labels each one:
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>State</th><th>Meaning</th><th>What to do</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>synced</code></td><td>Present on both sides</td><td>Nothing</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>app_manager_only</code></td><td>Here but not on Expo</td><td>Push it up with a sync</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>expo_only</code></td><td>On Expo but not here</td><td>Create it in <a href="/platform/applications">Applications</a></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Only iOS and Android take part; web identifiers have no meaning on Expo.
  </p>

  <h2>Credentials</h2>
  <p>
    EAS builds sign with credentials Expo holds, so the two vaults have to agree. Two operations bridge
    them:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Provision</strong> asks Expo to generate whatever signing material an identifier is
      missing.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Attach</strong> pushes a credential you already hold in the
      <a href="/platform/credentials-vault">vault</a> up to Expo.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Both move signing material between systems, so both require credential-management rights as well
    as integration rights.
  </p>

  <h2>EAS builds</h2>
  <p>
    An EAS build record mirrors an Expo build: you register it with the Expo build id, and the platform
    tracks its status. Manual status and log writes are refused, because Expo owns both — see
    <a href="/cli/builds/eas-builds">EAS builds</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    How quickly a mirrored record advances depends on sync. Without automatic sync it moves when
    something asks it to, which is why a finished Expo build can still read <code>pending</code> here.
  </p>

  <h2>Doing the same from the CLI</h2>
  <p>
    <a href="/cli/integrations/eas"><code>appmctl eas</code></a> covers all of it: connect,
    projects, import, link, status, sync, auto-sync, identifiers, sync-identifiers, provision, attach,
    disconnect. Actions that exist in both scopes take the scope from the flag —
    <code>--integration-id</code> for the account, <code>--app</code> for the project link.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
